pseudo-Boolean proof version 3.0
% Regression test (wrong verdict): `output DERIVABLE` trusted the core set to be
% input-entailed, which `solx` breaks.
%
% DERIVABLE claims every model of the input is a model of the output, and
% `check_file` establishes it by matching the output against the core set
% constraint for constraint -- sound only while every core constraint is
% entailed by the input. `solx` stores a satisfiability-preserving but NOT
% entailed constraint into core (its kernel taint is
% `solution-exclusion-unmodelled`), so after it the invariant is broken.
%
% Ground truth, one variable: the input `1 x1 >= 1` is satisfied by x1=1; the
% output `{1 x1 >= 1, 1 ~x1 >= 1}` is unsatisfiable. A satisfiable formula
% cannot entail an unsatisfiable one, so OUTPUT DERIVABLE is false -- yet this
% used to print `s VERIFIED OUTPUT DERIVABLE` at exit 0 under default flags, on
% this fork and on clean upstream 4bb10c2c, with `--kernel-verify` reporting no
% divergence.
%
% This is defect 15's failure mode at the one guarantee its fix did not reach:
% `EQUISATISFIABLE` and `EQUIENUMERABLE` already carry the `solution_logged`
% guard; the reasoning that DERIVABLE was "implication-based, so unaffected by
% weakened solution guarantees" silently assumed an input-entailed core.
% Invisible because no corpus proof ever wrote DERIVABLE.
%
% Found by the ninth red team, whose report arrived after a usage-limit
% interruption and was adjudicated on recovery.
f 1;
solx x1;
output DERIVABLE FILE;
conclusion NONE;
end pseudo-Boolean proof;
